## Context

Child of tracker 0613 (goal 1), successor to the dropped 0636. `Ticket-ref:
tickets/0613`, `Ticket-ref: tickets/0033` — neither closes; 0033 keeps its own
wider scope (ledger, freshness, pause, serve-stale, custody, query semantics),
this ticket is one phased slice of its counters part, scoped to what goal 1's
R22 needs and nothing past it.

**What SPEC.md §5.2.8 actually asks, distilled to what R22 needs.** The full
counter design is `work.<stage>.<trigger>.<outcome>`, stage ∈
`{record, extract, chunk, embed}` (§5.2.1), trigger ∈ `{new, edit, re-extract,
resync, key-bump, prefix-stale, retry, delete}`, outcome ∈ `{noop, done}`,
"updated in the same transaction as the ledger transition each one
describes." But `_pause_verdict` (`bench/acceptance/assertions.py` ~611-636,
the function that actually grades R22) only asks whether **any** `done`
counter advanced — it needs no trigger classification, which is why R22 can
be settled without the freshness half of the design (R3's counter-churn
clause is the one that needs the trigger axis, and R3 is goal 2). This ticket
exploits exactly that gap: implement enough of the real, durable counter
mechanism to answer "did embedding work happen" truthfully and persistently,
skip everything the freshness/custody/query-semantics parts of scoped issue A
would also need.

**What NOT to build here, and why it's out of scope even though SPEC.md
describes it.** §5.2.1/§5.2.2's `entries`/`slabs`/`passages`/`origins`/
`libraries` schema is this repository's own broader storage-redesign
vision — multi-table, content-addressed, built for a search-index-v2 this
project has reasoned about but never asked the maintainer to adopt wholesale
(0033's own log: "the maintainer picks the verbs," GOVERNANCE.md's asymmetry
rule). Zoteus's REAL schema today (verify fresh — see Actions) is far
smaller: a `passages` table with vector/text columns and a `meta` key-value
table already carrying a JSON `checkpoint` row (`sqlite-index.ts`
~928-1092). This ticket adds counters to that real, existing schema — it
does not redesign it.

**Why in-memory (last session's "Tier 1", `src/lib/metrics.ts`) is the
wrong tool despite being cheaper.** `metrics.ts`'s `createMetrics()`
registry (added upstream in `c2d83da`, unwired to the indexing pipeline) is
process-local and resets on restart. SPEC.md's own ask is durability
("the same transaction as the ledger transition"), and R22's second clause
is literally "holds across restart" — an in-memory counter can only ever
prove the FIRST clause (pause stops work) by accident of timing, never the
second with any rigor, since "the counter reset to 0 and stayed 0" is
indistinguishable from "the counter was never wired." The real fix has to
live in sqlite, committed atomically with whatever it counts.

## The phased design (Tier 2, in full)

Phase 0 and 1 are this ticket's own scope. Phases 2-4 are named so the shape
those add doesn't get invented twice, and so nothing here accidentally
forecloses them — they are NOT actions of this ticket.

**Phase 0 — the counters table and the transactional bump helper.**
A new sqlite table, e.g. `work_counters(stage TEXT, trigger TEXT, outcome
TEXT, count INTEGER, PRIMARY KEY(stage, trigger, outcome))`, added via the
existing migration-ladder mechanism (ticket #34's precedent,
`sqlite-index.ts`'s schema-version bump path — reuse it, don't invent a
second one). One helper, `bumpWorkCounter(db, stage, trigger, outcome, by=1)`,
an `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE SET count = count + ?`, called from
*inside* the same transaction as the write it describes — never as a
separate commit, which is exactly the drift SPEC.md's own `drift` counter
exists to catch (out of scope here; Phase 3).

**Phase 1 — embed-stage `done`, wired, exposed. This is the goal-1 slice.**
Call `bumpWorkCounter(db, 'embed', <trigger>, 'done', n)` at the point
`backfillVectors()`/`buildIncremental()` actually commits `n` newly-computed
vectors — re-verify the exact call site against live `upstream/main` (Action
1), but per the last reading it is inside the same `persistNow()`/`save()`
transaction the checkpoint already writes to (`index-manager.ts`
~1370-1381, ~1010-1012). Trigger can be a single, honest, coarse value for
this phase (e.g. always `"build"`) rather than the full 8-value vocabulary —
nothing downstream reads the trigger dimension yet, and a fabricated fine
classification would be worse than an honest coarse one. Expose the total
via `action:"status"`'s response as one new field, e.g. `workDone: {embed:
N}` or reuse the `metrics`-style shape SPEC.md's naming suggests — whichever
reads more naturally beside the fields `action:"status"`'s doc-comment
already documents (`index-tool.ts`).

**Phase 2 — record/extract/chunk stages, the real trigger vocabulary
(needed for R3, goal 2 — not this ticket).** Requires per-item key
comparison (§5.2.1's `record_hash`/`text_hash`/`(text_hash, segmenter,
chunker)` keys) to distinguish `resync.noop` from `*.done` — the actual
mechanism that would prove "an identical resync recomputes nothing." Zoteus's
real freshness check today is version-stamp/delta-based (`?since=`), not a
per-item content-hash comparison; building this phase means adding that
comparison, not just a counter.

**Phase 3 — drift detection and idle reconciliation.** Periodic recompute of
each counter from a real `COUNT(*)`, a `drift` counter the harness fails on
when it moves. Needed for R1's convergence harness and for the counters to
be self-defending against silent corruption; not needed for R22's two
clauses, which only ever read a fresh value.

**Phase 4 — custody, query semantics, outOfBand, the boundary cursor.** The
remainder of 0033's scoped issue A. Unrelated to the counter mechanism
mechanically; bundled historically because one upstream issue carries all of
it.

